<div id="S2.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G\cong\Gamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{\alpha}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G\cong\Gamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{\alpha}})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p=3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\alpha=5," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\alpha=5,</annotation></semantics></math> the independent domination polynomial is <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D_{i}(G,x)=2x+6x^{55}+x^{72}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>55</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>72</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{i}(G,x)=2x+6x^{55}+x^{72}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Example 3, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>) and the set of zeros of this polynomial is very long. So, we represent it by the graphical picture as shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ item 4 ‣ 2 Independent domination polynomial of zero divisor graphs of commutative rings ‣ A note on the Independent domination polynomial of zero divisor graph of rings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></p>
</div>
