<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p17.1" class="ltx_p">The following results gives the non unimodal and log-concave property of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Gamma(\mathbb{Z}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Gamma(\mathbb{Z}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n</annotation></semantics></math> is prime power.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n</annotation></semantics></math> is the prime power, then the independent domination polynomial of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Gamma(\mathbb{Z}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Gamma(\mathbb{Z}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave but not unimodal.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p18" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
Let <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n=p^{2m+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n=p^{2m+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p</annotation></semantics></math> is prime and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle m</annotation></semantics></math> is a positive integer. Then the independent domination polynomial of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G\cong\Gamma(\mathbb{Z}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G\cong\Gamma(\mathbb{Z}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
