<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p19.2" class="ltx_p">As in odd case, the exponents of the above expression are odd except <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\phi(p^{2m-1})+\phi(p^{2m-2})+\dots+\phi(p^{m+1})=p^{2m-1}-p^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\phi(p^{2m-1})+\phi(p^{2m-2})+\dots+\phi(p^{m+1})=p^{2m-1}-p^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and coefficients increase precisely <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle m+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle m+1</annotation></semantics></math> times. From the representation of coefficients and exponents of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D_{i}(G,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{i}(G,x)</annotation></semantics></math>, it is easy to see that <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1+p^{2m-1}-p^{2m-1-(i-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1+p^{2m-1}-p^{2m-1-(i-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1+p^{2m}-p^{2m-(i-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1+p^{2m}-p^{2m-(i-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> have large gaps for respective values of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle i." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle i.</annotation></semantics></math> Thus by definition, the log-concave property holds trivially.
∎</p>
